1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:04,287
Sixty birds.

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:08,887
Each one has a tiny brain with random weights, so none of them can fly.

3
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:12,887
Watch generation one: they all crash within a second or two.

4
00:00:13,167 --> 00:00:15,183
Now watch the best score climb.

5
00:00:15,463 --> 00:00:20,695
By generation eight, one bird flies the entire course, through every pipe.

6
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:22,847
Nobody taught it.

7
00:00:23,127 --> 00:00:28,983
There is no training data, no reward function in the usual sense, and no gradient descent.

8
00:00:29,263 --> 00:00:31,135
Just evolution.

9
00:00:31,415 --> 00:00:40,247
I'm going to show you the whole thing in about a hundred lines of Python, and I should say up front: I built it with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

10
00:00:41,047 --> 00:00:45,055
Most of the neural networks you hear about learn by gradient descent.

11
00:00:45,335 --> 00:00:49,535
You show them examples, measure the error, and nudge the weights downhill.

12
00:00:49,815 --> 00:00:54,543
That needs a smooth, differentiable signal and lots of labelled examples.

13
00:00:54,823 --> 00:00:56,863
But a game gives you something different.

14
00:00:57,143 --> 00:00:59,879
You don't have the right move for every frame.

15
00:01:00,159 --> 00:01:02,247
You only know how far the bird got.

16
00:01:02,527 --> 00:01:04,423
Neuroevolution uses that.

17
00:01:04,703 --> 00:01:11,615
Try a lot of random brains, keep the ones that got further, mix them, add some random changes, and repeat.

18
00:01:11,895 --> 00:01:17,487
It's the same idea as the rockets video, but now the thing being evolved is a neural network.

19
00:01:17,767 --> 00:01:19,639
This is an old technique.

20
00:01:19,919 --> 00:01:31,055
Variants of it were used to evolve controllers for robots and to play games long before deep reinforcement learning became popular, and it still turns up when the problem can't be differentiated.

21
00:01:31,855 --> 00:01:34,063
The world is deliberately small.

22
00:01:34,343 --> 00:01:37,127
A bird sits at a fixed horizontal position.

23
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:39,879
Gravity pulls it down every step.

24
00:01:40,159 --> 00:01:44,383
Each step the brain may flap, which sets its vertical speed upward.

25
00:01:44,663 --> 00:01:47,687
Pipes scroll past with a gap in each one.

26
00:01:47,967 --> 00:01:51,495
Hit a pipe, the floor or the ceiling and you are out.

27
00:01:51,775 --> 00:01:54,919
Your fitness is simply how many steps you survived.

28
00:01:55,199 --> 00:01:57,071
Here is the course.

29
00:01:57,351 --> 00:02:02,679
Every generation plays the same sequence of gaps, so scores can be compared fairly.

30
00:02:02,959 --> 00:02:04,831
And here is the brain.

31
00:02:05,111 --> 00:02:14,879
It has four inputs: how far the bird is below or above the middle of the next gap, its own height, its vertical speed, and the distance to the next pipe.

32
00:02:15,159 --> 00:02:18,471
One hidden layer of six units, and a single output.

33
00:02:18,751 --> 00:02:21,655
If the output is above zero, flap..

34
00:02:21,935 --> 00:02:27,791
That is the entire neural network: two matrix multiplications and two tanh functions.

35
00:02:28,071 --> 00:02:38,655
At the start every weight is a random number, so the bird's behaviour is random: some flap constantly and fly into the ceiling, some never flap and fall into the floor.

36
00:02:39,455 --> 00:02:41,903
Here is the loop that plays a generation..

37
00:02:42,183 --> 00:02:48,831
For every bird that is still alive: build its four inputs, ask the brain, flap if it says so.

38
00:02:49,111 --> 00:02:59,503
Then gravity, then movement, then collision: a bird is out if it leaves the screen, or if it overlaps a pipe horizontally while being outside the gap vertically.

39
00:02:59,783 --> 00:03:02,423
Anyone still alive gets one more point.

40
00:03:02,703 --> 00:03:06,759
That's the whole game, and it plays all sixty birds at the same time.

41
00:03:07,559 --> 00:03:09,431
Now the evolution step.

42
00:03:09,711 --> 00:03:13,551
Sort the birds by score, then build the next generation..

43
00:03:13,831 --> 00:03:18,511
The top ten percent are copied unchanged, so we never lose our best bird.

44
00:03:18,791 --> 00:03:21,743
The parents for everyone else come from the top third.

45
00:03:22,023 --> 00:03:28,647
For crossover, each weight in the child is copied from one parent or the other, chosen by a coin flip.

46
00:03:28,927 --> 00:03:34,783
For mutation, each weight has a chance, the mutation rate, of getting a random nudge.

47
00:03:35,063 --> 00:03:38,567
That's it: selection, crossover, mutation.

48
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:46,087
I leave the defaults: sixty birds, twenty generations at most, mutation point two, seed one.

49
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:48,239
I press Evolve.

50
00:03:48,519 --> 00:03:54,207
The app plays each generation live, with all the living birds drawn on top of each other.

51
00:03:54,487 --> 00:04:00,511
The counter shows how many are still alive, and the chart on the right shows the best score of each generation.

52
00:04:00,791 --> 00:04:05,399
Generation one: the best bird survives only eighty-eight steps.

53
00:04:05,679 --> 00:04:08,775
Generations two and three barely improve.

54
00:04:09,055 --> 00:04:16,855
Then at generation four the best score jumps to four hundred and eighty-five, because a lucky child found how to get through the first few pipes.

55
00:04:17,135 --> 00:04:27,599
It stays there for two generations, then climbs to one thousand and sixty-two, and in generation eight a bird survives all twelve hundred steps, the full course.

56
00:04:27,879 --> 00:04:31,383
The app stops there and says the population has learned to play.

57
00:04:31,663 --> 00:04:35,815
The shape of that chart is typical: flat for a while, then a jump.

58
00:04:36,095 --> 00:04:41,663
Evolution doesn't improve smoothly; it waits for a lucky combination and then keeps it.

59
00:04:42,463 --> 00:04:44,335
How little the brain needs.

60
00:04:44,615 --> 00:04:46,511
Six hidden units were enough.

61
00:04:46,791 --> 00:04:56,703
And how much tuning the game itself took: my first version was so easy that a random bird already flew the whole course, and my second was so hard that nothing evolved.

62
00:04:56,983 --> 00:04:59,143
The final settings sit in between.

63
00:04:59,423 --> 00:05:03,119
Making the game fair mattered as much as the evolution code.

64
00:05:03,399 --> 00:05:05,415
Also how much the seed matters.

65
00:05:05,695 --> 00:05:12,943
With the same settings, seed two solves it in three generations, seed three needs seven and seed four needs eight.

66
00:05:13,743 --> 00:05:15,615
This is a toy.

67
00:05:15,895 --> 00:05:25,327
The course is the same every generation, so the birds learn this one sequence of gaps, not Flappy Bird in general; I haven't tested them on new courses.

68
00:05:25,607 --> 00:05:28,126
The score is capped at twelve hundred steps.

69
00:05:28,407 --> 00:05:35,031
The simulation is simplified: no real Flappy Bird physics, and the pipes are evenly spaced.

70
00:05:35,311 --> 00:05:40,231
And every number here comes from a handful of runs, so don't read them as a benchmark.

71
00:05:40,511 --> 00:05:45,862
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but you should read it yourself too.

72
00:05:46,662 --> 00:05:52,374
Next I'd generate a fresh course for every generation and see whether the brains generalise.

73
00:05:52,655 --> 00:05:56,758
I'd also like to add a second kind of obstacle, or let you draw the course.

74
00:05:57,039 --> 00:06:01,551
And a comparison with gradient-based reinforcement learning would be a good video.

75
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:06,126
The code is in the description, no API key, no cost.

76
00:06:06,407 --> 00:06:13,103
If you enjoyed watching birds learn, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which game I should evolve next.
